WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:03.800
Although stars appear as simple

00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:06.280
points of light in the sky,

00:00:06.310 --> 00:00:08.080
astronomers now know that almost all of them

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:10.420
are orbited by planets.

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:12.510
Even without being able to see them,

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:15.460
researchers have discovered whole planetary systems

00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:18.320
and have measured the sizes, masses, and orbits

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:23.660
of the planets -- some tantalizingly similar to Earth.

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:25.950
But do any of them harbor life?

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:27.700
Answering that question seems impossible

00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:30.920
since the planets are too far and too faint to see.

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:34.080
Astronomers may soon find an answer by exploiting

00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:37.180
the power of spectroscopy: spreading out the various

00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:39.980
wavelengths of light prismatically to see which ones

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:42.579
are brightest and which might have been absorbed

00:00:42.580 --> 00:00:44.300
by something on their way here.

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:47.070
When a planet passes in front of its star,

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:48.899
some of the starlight arriving to telescopes

00:00:48.899 --> 00:00:51.379
will have passed through the planet’s atmosphere

00:00:51.379 --> 00:00:54.920
and gases will have absorbed certain wavelengths.

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:57.070
Space telescopes such as Hubble are using this method

00:00:57.070 --> 00:00:59.300
to detect gases around exoplanets.

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:02.170
For now they’ve only managed this with a
few gas giants,

00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:04.380
not around anything like Earth.

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:08.420
To find more constituents of exoplanet atmospheres,

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:11.620
astronomers need to block out the light from the star

00:01:11.630 --> 00:01:13.430
so that they can see the dim planets

00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:15.590
and gather their light directly.

00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:17.909
That can be done with a mask inside the telescope

00:01:17.909 --> 00:01:19.120
called a coronagraph

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:21.700
but far more effective will be a starshade.

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:25.000
This is a free-flying spacecraft which can unfurl

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:28.080
a parasol tens of meters across.

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:30.650
When stationed tens of thousands of kilometers away

00:01:30.650 --> 00:01:32.000
from a space telescope,

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:33.840
it can block the light from a star

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:36.020
while leaving planets in view.

00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:38.399
Astronomers hope such space missions will happen

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:39.440
in the next decade.

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:41.420
But once we can see exoplanets

00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:44.020
directly what signs should we look for to tell us

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:45.729
that life is present?

00:01:45.729 --> 00:01:48.649
What are the biosignatures?

00:01:48.649 --> 00:01:51.000
If we take Earth life as a guide, then looking

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:53.820
for the byproducts of biological processes, such as

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:57.100
oxygen and methane, seems like a good approach.

00:01:57.680 --> 00:01:59.780
These gases would normally react and turn into

00:01:59.799 --> 00:02:02.260
something else, so if they are detected in large

00:02:02.260 --> 00:02:05.570
quantities it is a sign that something, such as life,

00:02:05.570 --> 00:02:07.070
is replenishing them.

00:02:07.070 --> 00:02:08.929
But it’s easy to be fooled.

00:02:08.929 --> 00:02:09.929
Methane can be produced

00:02:09.929 --> 00:02:13.840
by volcanoes; oxygen by ultraviolet light splitting water.

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:16.660
Similarly, it is possible for life to thrive

00:02:16.660 --> 00:02:18.400
without producing such gases,

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:21.040
as microbes did on the primitive Earth

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:22.620
for billions of years.

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:25.280
Some scientists argue that we shouldn’t be looking out

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:28.530
for a duplicate of Earth-based life but should consider

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:31.720
all volatile compounds as possible biosignatures.

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:33.840
Any atmosphere that is out of balance,

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:36.820
with constant replenishment of reactive gases,

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:39.340
could be flagging up an alien biology.

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:41.420
Astronomers now realize that detecting

00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:42.740
atmospheric gases

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:46.380
is unlikely to be enough to convincingly detect life.

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:49.940
They will need a holistic view of a planet:

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:52.800
What sort of light rains down from its star?

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:56.010
does it have volcanoes? How fast does it rotate?

00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:57.790
The light reflected from its surface could show

00:02:57.790 --> 00:03:00.900
a pattern of absorption like that from our green

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:02.850
photosynthetic plants.

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:05.640
With the help of starshades and giant space telescopes,

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:09.060
one day we’ll get images of such a distant world.

00:03:09.070 --> 00:03:11.920
They may be only a few pixels across but researchers

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:14.810
have simulated them using degraded images of Earth

00:03:14.810 --> 00:03:17.720
and believe they will be able to detect oceans

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:20.980
and continents, rotation rate and seasons.

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:22.100
From these they can get

00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:26.320
a better idea of a planet’s climate and suitability for life.

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:27.520
Take a close look.

00:03:27.820 --> 00:03:30.860
A picture like this may be our first glimpse of a world

00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:34.340
with life that has evolved separately from our own.

